      "writtenOn": "2021-10-06T14:33:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I guess the one case where the sender/receiver confusion could be interesting from a security perspective would be if the victim process is a privileged executable (like crash_dump or runas) that was previously executing in a less trusted context, and did an execute-transition into a more privileged context. In that case an attacker might have opened another /dev/binder instance for the victim process before the execute-transition, so if the victim process then looked up an attacker-supplied binder (which is owned by the attacker-created /dev/binder instance), and did a transaction with an FDA to it, and that transaction failed, then you\u0027d hit this bug and incorrectly close FDs in the victim process, I believe? Which would then lead to file descriptor lifetime issues.\n\nThere aren\u0027t many execute-transitions on Android where privileges get increased though (runas and crash_dump are the only important examples I can think of), and I don\u0027t think they use binder very much.\n\nSo I\u0027d probably count that one as a bit risky in terms of security, but probably not currently exploitable?",
      "parentUuid": "9c0fbca1_78020475",
      "revId": "65d041497cce869675a6339c17f037bab1ec2998",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
